1. Introduction
Biological cells contain droplets, known as biomolecular condensates, which partition the cells’ interior without using lipid membranes [1]. Besides simply structuring cells, these droplets also provide distinct chemical environments [2], affect reactions [3, 4], sequester molecules [5], exert forces [6],
and can induce phenotypic switching [7]. Droplets are implicated in homeostasis of healthy cells (e.g. in development [8], gene regulation [9, 10], protein quality control [11], and signalling [12]), but also in diseased states [13, 14] (e.g. neurodegenerative diseases [15], and cancer [16]). It is thus not surprising that droplets appear everywhere in cells, from the nucleus [17], to the cytosol [1], to membranes [12], and in all forms of life, encompassing bacteria [18], plants [19], and animals [1]. While it is clear that droplets are crucial for cells, it is less clear how they form and how cells control them. The physics of droplet formation, known as phase separation, has been studied for more than a century [20], including in biological contexts [21]. Phase separation generally describes how a balance between entropy, favouring mixing, and enthalpy, due to interactions, leads to stable droplets in thermodynamic equilibrium. While this basic principle likely also governs droplet formation in cells [22], phase separation in a biological context is significantly more complex for at least three major reasons (figure 1). First, cells comprise an enormous number of different biomolecules, which can each be incredibly complex [23]. Consequently, droplets typically comprise many components and posses complex material properties. Second, the cellular environment is heterogeneous, filled with membranes, rigid polymers, and other macromolecular structures. Droplets interact with this environment through wetting, which affects both the droplets and the environment itself. Third, cells are alive, implying that processes can be driven away from equilibrium, which can profoundly affect droplet formation [24]. One challenge in describing cellular droplets theoretically lies in the many different length scales involved: microscopic interactions (e.g. π–π-stacking, cation-π interactions, hydrogen bonding, and hydrophobic interactions [25]) induce phase separation; mesoscopic interactions (e.g. complex coacervation [26], entanglement, and gelation) determine mechanical properties of droplets; and macroscopic interactions (e.g. gradients, flow, and wetting) control droplets on the cellular scale. Theory is necessary to bridge these scales and to arrive at a holistic description of the physical processes that shape droplets in cells. This review summarises recent work on intracellular phase separation, focusing on the physical processes that allow cells to regulate droplets, i.e. processes that determine when and where droplets form, how large they get, and when they dissolve. We focus less on how droplets affect cellular processes in vivo (reviewed in [1, 4, 6, 8–19, 23, 25, 27–62]) or in related in vitro studies (reviewed in [9, 26, 61–81]). We also neglect external influences, such as electric and magnetic fields, or gravity, which is only relevant in extremely large cells [82], and do not discuss the detailed (bio-)chemistry that leads to phase separation (see [23, 83–86] for details). Instead, we assume that such interactions can be captured by a suitable coarse-grained free energy [87], so that we can describe droplets using continuous concentration fields. Our approach is similar to that taken in other reviews of the theory of phase
