2.3. Passive control of droplet life cycle
The basic physics of phase separation discussed so far already provides ample opportunity for control of droplet life cycles. Droplets form by nucleation when the mixture is supersaturated, and then grow by taking up material from the dilute phase. Multiple droplets compete against each other for this material, resulting in a coarsening process that favours larger droplets, so that a single droplet remains in the final equilibrium state. The size of this droplet is a function of global parameters, which can also vary in space and time.
2.3.1. Global parameters. Droplet size is directly influ
enced by the amount of droplet material in the system, φ ̄Vsys; see equation (2.42). Cells can thus influence whether droplets form and how large they get by producing and degrading the relevant droplet material. Droplet size also depends on the coexisting volume fractions φ(0)
out and φ(0)
in , which in turn
depend on the interaction χ and the molecular size Ω; see equation (2.21). These aspects can be influenced by modifying the involved molecules, either by post-translational modifications [36, 54] or by multimerisation [172], which can induce phase separation in vitro [173] and in vivo [14, 174]. The interactions between molecules are also sensitive to environmental influences [72], including temperature [175–177], pH [178], osmotic conditions [179], crowding conditions [69], CO2 concentration [180], and electric fields [181]. Cells can exploit the phase transition associated with changes in these environmental parameters to deploy appropriate responses, e.g. to control flowering in plants [182]. There is also evidence that evolution adapts genetic sequences to different temperature niches to control this behaviour [183].
2.3.2. Spatial gradients. The sensitivity of phase separation to environmental parameters provides exquisite control over droplet positions when external gradients are involved. For instance, compositional gradients can bias droplets toward one side of a system [116, 184–187], for both passive [150, 188] and active droplets [189], in a process akin to diffusiophoresis [190]. In sufficiently large cells, gravity can also become important, so that droplets sediment due to density mismatch [82]. Finally, artificially induced temperature gradients can position droplets [175], although such gradients are likely negligible in most cells [103].
2.3.3. Temporal variations. Global parameters can also vary in time. For instance, the external environment can exhibit wet–dry cycles [191] or temperature oscillations, which need to be compensated in circadian rhythms [192]. Alternatively, cells can generate oscillations internally to affect condensates [193]. These oscillatory drives can in particular influence chemical reactions controlled by droplets [194]. Besides structured variations of global parameters, cells are also subject to
fluctuations, e.g. from gene expression. In the simple case of binary mixtures, these fluctuations can be buffered by phase separation since the equilibrium concentrations are independent of the total amount of material [195]. Buffering effects persist in multi-component mixtures, although concentration buffering may be weaker [196, 197]. On the other hand, volume buffering is also then possible [198].
3. Beyond simple liquid droplets
Cells contain an endless variety of biomolecules. For example, there are an estimated 105 different proteins [199] present in the cell. It is thus not surprising that condensates typically consist of many different types of molecules [55, 200– 202], many of which have complex individual properties themselves. Molecules can also form partially ordered structures within droplets, such as filaments [203] and porous networks [204, 205]. The complex compositional features resulting from this mixture of biomolecules affects both the bulk and interfacial properties of droplets, and also enables the coexistence of multiple different types of droplet within a cell.
3.1. Complex molecular interactions
Biomolecular condensates contain a huge variety of components, including water, ions, small peptides, proteins and flexible polymers such as RNA and DNA [23, 63]. Since it is challenging to directly study interactions between these molecules in their native environment, much experimental work has thus far relied on in vitro reconstitution [38, 206–208]. Here, it is crucial to exploit label-free techniques [209, 210] since fluorescent labels typically strongly affect droplet condensation [210, 211]. One key insight from these approaches is that often a few scaffold components control the formation of condensates, while client molecules partition into them [212– 216]. However, this dichotomy is likely an oversimplification and it will be interesting to more rigorously quantify the contributions of individual components, using theoretical analysis [217–219] or the recently developed dominance metric [220], which can uncover basic interaction motifs between components [221]. It is clear that biomolecular phase separation involves many different kinds of interactions between molecules [222]. To distill the essential features of these interactions, it is often helpful to coarse-grain particular microscopic models [93, 223]. For instance, many biomolecules can be conceptualised as associative polymers [85, 222, 224–227] and described by sticker-and-spacer models [23, 86, 224, 228, 229], where flexible spacers link stickers with specific interactions. However, real biomolecules are complex and thus require flexible modelling approaches, which are reviewed in [23, 63, 90, 230]. Such models allow elucidating how particular molecular features, like the amino acid sequence of proteins, affect condensate properties [231–234], and can explain why dilute phases often exhibit surprisingly large clusters [235, 236]. Interactions between biomolecules can be roughly divided into weak, transient interactions and strong, persistent bonds.
11


Rep. Prog. Phys. 88 (2025) 116601 Review
While weak multivalent interactions are generally responsible for phase separation into liquid-like phases, strong interactions tend to form long-lived assemblies, which can persist as nano-domains inside condensates [237]. Moreover, chemical modifications of biomolecules can affect their interactions. For instance, proteins can be changed by post-translational modifications [199], which are implicated in condensate regulation [36, 54, 238, 239]. Such changes can induce multimerisation, which can in turn enable phase separation. To understand this phenomenon, we imagine a molecule whose weak interactions are insufficient for phase separation. A multimer of such molecules, however, can interact with many other multimers, which effectively increases the molecular volume Ω while maintaining the interaction strength χ per unit volume [172]. This process essentially reduces the entropic cost of concentrating molecules, promoting phase separation. This feature has been used to induce artificial condensates in cells [14, 240]. More generally, it is likely that combinations of weak and strong interactions enable fine-tuning of condensate properties [241, 242].
3.2. Complex material properties
The complex nature of the constituent biomolecules also results in condensates exhibiting complex rheological properties that cannot be captured by simple (Newtonian) droplet models [136], due to long, flexible polymers deforming, entangling, and interacting with each other. Cells exploit these complex material properties to carry out function [243]: for example, centrosomes are able to grow rapidly in a fluid-like manner, but also exhibit solid-like properties to support large stresses during cell division [244, 245].
3.2.1. Internal stresses. The formation, and subsequent growth, of droplets induces stresses both in the droplets themselves and in the surrounding solvent. These stresses may then give rise to hydrodynamic flows (equation (2.9)) that in turn affect the composition of the droplet–solvent mixture (equation (2.8)). As such, there is a strong two-way coupling between hydrodynamics and droplet regulation, via the stress field: droplets can induce flows, which then advect and/or deform the droplets themselves. Recent numerical works have highlighted how this interplay can accelerate droplet coarsening [128, 246], as well as affect the morphology of intermediate droplet states [128]. For a single-component Newtonian fluid, the total stress can be decomposed into contributions arising from hydrostatic pressure and viscous dissipation [121]. The hydrostatic pressure p enforces incompressibility of the fluid, and is determined by momentum conservation (Navier–Stokes; equation (2.9)) and the incompressibility condition ∇ · v = 0. Multi-component fluids additionally induce an equilibrium stress, σeq, that captures osmotic and interfacial effects. Further, many biological fluids exhibit a complex rheology that results in an elastic contribution to the stress, denoted by σel. The overall stress tensor σ then comprises the sum of each
