deformations of different frequency ω. The storage modulus, G′, describes in-phase (solid-like) responses to deformation, and the loss modulus, G′′, describes out-of-phase (liquidlike) responses. For low frequency (slow) deformations, the loss modulus is larger than the storage modulus, and viscous effects dominate. For high frequency (fast) deformations, the storage modulus is larger than the loss modulus, and elastic effects dominate. The two curves intersect at the characteristic frequency ωc = τ −1. The viscoelastic material parameters of condensates can be either measured experimentally [136], or predicted via molecular dynamics simulations [253, 254]. Tanaka and coworkers [255–257] provide a comprehensive overview of the coupling between phase separation and viscoelasticity. Viscoelastic properties have been shown to stabilise droplets against dissolution [258], provide resistance to external stress, and allow the formation of complex, domainspanning droplet morphologies [257]. Viscoelasticity also has a strong effect on droplet fusion time. For small τ , fusion time increases relative to the Newtonian case, whereas for large τ , fusion time decreases [259]. The viscoelastic properties of condensates may change over time, in a process known as ageing [250, 251, 260–262]. During ageing, condensates progressively become less able to deform, with an increase in relaxation time leading to a more solid-like rheology. The molecular mechanisms that drive condensate ageing are currently an active topic of research [13, 263, 264], although many theoretical works model ageing condensates as soft glassy systems. Aged condensates take longer to recover their shape after deformation and show suppressed ability to coalesce [265]. It is also possible for aged condensates to form complex droplet architectures, in which different parts of the droplet display distinct mechanical properties [266]. Note that the storage and loss moduli of aged condensates at different time points often collapse onto a single curve when scaled by their (time-dependent) characteristic frequency ωc [251, 263, 267].
3.2.3. Gelation. Liquid droplets are characterised by their dynamic, amorphous structure and ability to dissolve in a reversible manner. It is possible, however, for condensates to undergo a phase transition to a gel-like state [14, 242, 268, 269]. This transition results in kinetic arrest, in which condensates maintain their amorphous structure but lose their dynamic properties [270–272]. Surprisingly, gelation can lead to enhanced ripening relative to liquid droplets [273]. A further transition can result in the formation of irreversible fibrous aggregates, which are linked to the development of various neurodegenerative conditions [13, 28]. Recent work has shown that gelation typically initiates at the interface between the droplet and its surroundings, propagating inwards, such that droplets transiently form a core–shell geometry with a liquid phase surrounded by a gel phase [274]. Similarly, fast changes in composition can result in the formation of kinetically arrested double emulsion structures within droplets [275]. Gelation and aggregation are enhanced in droplets compared to dilute proteins as a result of the increased protein concentration [276, 277], however the formation of metastable
non-fibrillar condensates can also suppress aggregation [278]. Gelation can also be driven by shear flows which help to align protein molecules into a kinematically-arrested orientation [279].
3.3. Complex interfaces
Interfaces between droplets and the surrounding fluid are characterised by a sharp change in composition over a finite spatial width. Typically, a rapid and continuous exchange of components between droplets and the surrounding fluid occurs across the interface, even at equilibrium. Since droplets in biological cells generally comprise long, complex molecules, however, this exchange may be limited by an interface resistance. Such a resistance could result from potential barriers, a reduction in diffusivity [280], saturation of binding sites [281], or electrostatic effects [282]. Many proteins molecules are amphiphilic, and can thus act as surfactants, preferentially associating to the interface [283– 286]. In general, surfactants stabilise droplet interfaces, resisting both dissolution and coalescence. The formation of complex interfaces via preferential protein association can therefore improve structural integrity of condensates and assist in size regulation [287], as well as leading to dynamical anisotropies at the interface [288]. Note that although surfactants usually result in kinetic droplet stabilisation, some complex particles may also result in thermodynamic stabilisation to form Pickering emulsions [161, 289, 290]. The amphiphilic properties of protein molecules depend on their particular conformational state [291]. Correspondingly, the chemical switching of a particular protein from a surfactant to liquid-like form has been implicated in chromosome clustering during mitotic exit [292]. Further, interfaces themselves can alter protein conformations, typically leading to extended molecular configurations [293]. Variations in either surfactant configuration or compositional state along an interface can give rise to surface tension gradients. These gradients drive Marangoni flows at the surface of the droplet, which may then lead to droplet swimming [68, 294, 295], thus highlighting how complex interfacial properties are able to affect the behaviour of the whole droplet.
