5. Chemically active droplets
Cells can control the properties of biomolecules by chemical modifications, such as post-translational modifications in the case of proteins. Such modifications can affect physical interactions with other biomolecules and thus also phase separation. Many condensates in cells are regulated by chemical modifications [44, 54, 238, 239, 433], including centrosomes [434] and stress granules [435]. To develop a physical understanding of these regulatory mechanisms, we conceptualise biomolecular modifications as chemical transitions between two forms of the same molecule, differing in their interaction parameters encoded by wi and χij. In this section, we discuss the complex interplay between chemical reactions and phase separation. After introducing a general framework for describing (active) chemical reactions, we couple reactions to phase separation, identifying two distinct classes of chemically active droplets in the simplest case of a binary mixture.
