5.5. More complex chemically active droplets
In this section, we have presented a continuous theory of binary chemically active droplets, which neglects many molecular details of the biomolecules involved. Including such details, even for simple effects such as molecular crowding [481], can lead to additional complex behaviour [482]. Similarly, when many interacting species are involved, we expect to find further interesting effects. In the future, it will thus be important to study thermodynamically consistent models of chemically active multicomponent mixtures, as well as how these mixtures interact with their environment. Since this topic represents a relatively new field of research, we highlight only a few recent discoveries in this direction below.
5.5.1. Chemically active wetting. Chemically active droplets typically do not exist in isolation but interact with their surrounding. While such interactions naturally lead to the wetting phenomena discussed in sections 4 and 5.3.3, the additional fluxes created by chemical reactions can also distort the droplet; see figure 12 for an example. The electrostatic analogy introduced in section 5.3.2 explains this deformation for externally maintained droplets since the overall energy is lowered by spatially separating parts of positively charged droplets [464]. Deformed droplets can also emerge when the wetted structure enriches catalytic enzymes, so that reactions themselves become localised, which further distorts the diffusive fluxes [463] and can position the droplet relative to the wetted structure [454]. Complex interactions between wetted structures and reactions thus allow for an intricate control over when and where condensates appear [483].
